      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nCertificate details:\n- Certificate No.: R49/2013-CZ-16.04, Revision 1\n- Member state: Czech Republic\n- Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, CZ\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Applicant: Arkon Flow Systems, s.r.o., Berkova 534/92, 612 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Manufacturer: Arkon Flow Systems, s.r.o. (same address)\n- Certified type: Water meter, Type MAGX2\n- Recommendation: R 49, edition 2013, accuracy class 2\n- Date issued: 12 October 2017 (signed by Pavel Klenovský, OIML Issuing Authority)\n- Page total: 8 pages\n\nTest reports:\n1. No. 6015-PT-P3021-16, from 17th August 2016, 240 pages\n2. No. 8551-PT-E0122-16, from 30th May 2016, 42 pages\n3. No. 8551-PT-E0142-16, from 10th July 2016, 48 pages\n4. No. 6015-PT-P3039-17, from 12th September 2017, 23 pages\n5. No. 8553-PT-P1005-17, from 7th February 2017, 6 pages\n6. No. 8551-PT-E0152-17, from 12th July 2017, 44 pages\n7. No. 8551-PT-E0153-17, from 27th August 2017, 44 pages\n\nDescription: electromagnetic water meter, compact and remote versions, cold and hot potable water, PTFE and hard rubber lining, straight inlet 5×D and outlet 3×D, no flow conditioner, electronic calculating/indicating device, max cable length remote 6 m, display in m³ volume and m³/h flow rate, not designed for reverse flow, etc.\n\nModels: The certificate covers MAGX2 in DN25 to DN300 — 12 nominal diameters: 25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300 mm.\n\nThis is clearly a model matrix. Models identified by DN. Model family: MAGX2, models: DN25, DN32, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300.\n\nCharacteristics varying per model:\n- Nominal diameter (DN) [mm]: 25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300\n- Length L [mm]: 200 (DN25-100), 250 (DN125, DN150), 300 (DN150?)... wait let me check.\n\n\"Length of horizontal water meter L [mm]\" — colspan 6 = 200 (DN25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80), colspan 2 = 250 (DN100? DN125?), then 300 (DN150?). Hmm.\n\nActually the first table covers DN25 TO DN150 with columns: 25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150 (9 columns). The Length row: colspan=6 → 200 covers columns 1-6 (DN25-80); colspan=2 → 250 covers columns 7-8 (DN100, DN125); colspan=1 → 300 covers column 9 (DN150).\n\nHmm, but that's unusual. Typically DN100 and DN125 have length 250? Let me think. Actually the standard lengths: DN25=200? Hmm, typically flanged water meters have lengths per ISO 4064: DN25... Actually lengths: 200mm for DN25-DN80? DN100-125: 250mm, DN150: 300mm. That matches typical ISO lengths. OK.\n\nSecond table DN200-300: Length L: 350 (DN200), 400 (DN250), 500 (DN300). Each colspan=1.\n\nIndicating range [m³]: first table: colspan 4 = 99999 (DN25, 32, 40, 50), colspan 5 = 999999 (DN65-150). Second table: 9999999 for DN200-300.\n\nFlowrates per DN (from page 5 table). There are 6 blocks, each for a Q3/Q1 ratio: 400, 250, 200, 160, 100, 50. Each block gives Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 for each DN.\n\nQ3 values are the same across all ratio blocks per DN: DN25: 16.0, DN32: 25.0, DN40: 40.0, DN50: 63.0, DN65: 100.0, DN80: 160.0, DN100: 250.0, DN125: 400.0, DN150: 630.0, DN200: 1000.0, DN250: 1000.0, DN300: 1600.0.\n\nQ4 = 1.25 × Q3: 20.0, 31.3, 50.0, 78.8, 125.0, 200.0, 312.5, 500.0, 787.5, 1250.0, 1250.0, 2000.0.\n\nQ2 = 1.6 × Q1 in all blocks. Q1 varies by ratio block.\n\nRatio Q3/Q1 options: 400, 250, 200, 160, 100, 50 (per DN selection).\n\nQ2/Q1 ratio: 1.6. Q4/Q3: 1.25.\n\nOther type-level characteristics:\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- MPE lower zone: ±5%\n- MPE upper zone: ±2% for water ≤30°C; ±3% for water >30°C\n- Temperature class: T50\n- Pressure-loss class: ΔP 10\n- Resolution of indicating device [m³]: 0.001 (normal mode), 0.000001 (calibration mode)\n- Flow profile sensitivity classes: U5 D3\n- Orientation limitation: any\n- Connection type: flange\n- Climatic environment class: B\n- Electromagnetic environment class: E2\n- Software version: 21.39\n- Checksum: 09585\n- Power supply: (90–250) VAC / 50 Hz; (12–36) VDC\n- Low flow cut off: 1% from nominal flowrate\n\nNow for matrix tables: the big flowrates table on page 5 is a multi-dimensional table: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 × DN × ratio. I should preserve it as matrix_tables with rows.\n\nI'll emit:\n1. matrix_tables: \"Basic technical data by nominal diameter\" — columns: nominal_diameter_mm, indicating_range_m3, length_mm... Actually maybe two tables:\n   - \"Basic technical data by nominal diameter (DN25–DN150)\" rows for each DN with length and indicating range.\n   - \"Basic technical data by nominal diameter (DN200–DN300)\".\n   - \"Flowrates by nominal diameter and Q3/Q1 ratio\" — this is the page-5 table.\n\nFor the flowrates table, structure: rows keyed by ratio and Qx. I could flatten: rows like {\"nominal_diameter\": 25, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 400, \"q1\": 0.04, \"q2\": 0.06, \"q3\": 16.0, \"q4\": 20.0}. That's a nice normalization. Columns: nominal_diameter (DN), ratio_q3_q1, q1_m3_h, q2_m3_h, q3_m3_h, q4_m3_h. 12 DN × 6 ratios = 72 rows. That's a lot but it preserves data faithfully. Alternatively keep the table in original shape: name \"Flowrates\", columns [ratio_q3_q1, then q1..q4 for each of 12 DN]. Hmm, the normalized flat format is clearer and machine-friendly. 72 rows is verbose but acceptable. Let me do it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. The flowrate data also feeds model_level characteristics? The main axis is DN (model) and ratio (configuration axis). Q3 and Q4 are per-model (constant across ratios). Q1 and Q2 vary per model AND per ratio — that's config_level: attribute q1, axis \"ratio_q3_q1\", values keyed by condition like \"DN25 ratio 400\". Hmm.\n\nThe config_level values format: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. So for attribute \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", axis \"q3_q1_ratio\", values: [{condition: \"DN25, ratio 400\", value: 0.04}, ...]. That would be 72 entries per attribute. Alternatively put it all in matrix_tables and provide model_level for q3/q4/DN/length/indicating range, and config_level for q1/q2.\n\nI think the cleanest:\n- model_family.models: 12 models DN25...DN300.\n- model_level characteristics:\n  - nominal_diameter: values per model (mm)\n  - permanent_flowrate_q3: per model (m³/h)\n  - overload_flowrate_q4: per model (m³/h)\n  - length: per model (mm)\n  - indicating_range: per model (m³)\n- config_level:\n  - minimum_flowrate_q1: axis \"q3_q1_ratio\", values with condition \"DN25 ratio 400\" etc. — 72 entries\n  - transitional_flowrate_q2: same, 72 entries\n\nThat's a lot of JSON but correct. Alternatively, I could rely on matrix_tables for the full table and keep model_level smaller. The rules say emit model_level when attributes vary by model. Q1/Q2 vary by both model and ratio — config_level is the right place with axis describing the dimension, and condition encoding both. Hmm, but axis is a single dimension description. Condition can be \"DN25 | ratio 400\".\n\nActually maybe better: treat ratio as part of model_id? No — DN is the model.\n\nAlternatively, I could define model_id as \"MAGX2 DN25 R400\" etc.? That'd be 12×6 = 72 models. Hmm, the certificate says \"Ratio Q3/Q1: 400 or 250 or 200 or 160 or 100 or 50\" — these are options per meter, i.e., configuration. The flowrate table lists values for each combination. I think treating ratio as a configuration axis is most faithful.\n\nTo keep the output manageable, I'll put the full per-combination data in matrix_tables (one flat table with 72 rows), and also emit:\n- model_level: nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, length, indicating_range\n- config_level: minimum_flowrate_q1 and transitional_flowrate_q2 with axis \"q3_q1_ratio\", conditions like \"DN25; R400\".\n\nActually 72×2 config entries is huge. Let me think about whether to include them. The schema example for R137 populated model_level with qmax/qt/qmin from the matrix table. Following that spirit, I should populate q1/q2 too. But 144 entries... it's verbose but machine-parseable. Alternatively, since matrix_tables already contain the full data, maybe model_level for q3/q4 and config_level for q1/q2 grouped per DN: condition \"ratio 400\" ... no wait, config_level values need to distinguish model too.\n\nHmm. Option: For config_level, use condition strings like \"DN25, Q3/Q1=400\". 72 entries per attribute. Two attributes → 144 entries. That's a lot of JSON, but it's faithful. Alternatively I can restrict to matrix_tables only for q1/q2 and note in description. The rules say \"Detect this and put varying attributes in model_level, NOT type_level\" — the main concern is not flattening to type_level. Putting them in matrix_tables preserves them. I think including config_level for q1/q2 is the most schema-conformant. Let me include them.\n\nWait — actually, is there another possibility: treat each (DN, ratio) as a distinct model variant? The certified type covers meters with selectable ratio... no, the ratio is fixed per meter at manufacture presumably (marked R400 or R250...). So each sold meter is DN + ratio. That suggests models = 72 combos. But that seems overkill; DN is the physical size model. I'll go with 12 models + config axis for ratio.\n\nLet me now carefully transcribe the flowrate table:\n\nRatio 400 block:\nQ1: 0.04, 0.06, 0.10, 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.63, 1.00, 1.58, 2.50, 2.50, 4.00\nQ2: 0.06, 0.10, 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.64, 1.00, 1.60, 2.52, 4.00, 4.00, 6.40\nQ3: 16.0, 25.0, 40.0, 63.0, 100.0, 160.0, 250.0, 400.0, 630.0, 1000.0, 1000.0, 1600.0\nQ4: 20.0, 31.3, 50.0, 78.8, 125.0, 200.0, 312.5, 500.0, 787.5, 1250.0, 1250.0, 2000.0\n\nRatio 250 block:\nQ1: 0.06, 0.10, 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.64, 1.00, 1.60, 2.52, 4.00, 4.00, 6.40\nQ2: 0.10, 0.16, 0.26, 0.40, 0.64, 1.02, 1.60, 2.56, 4.03, 6.40, 6.40, 10.24\nQ3: same as above\nQ4: same\n\nRatio 200 block:\nQ1: 0.08, 0.13, 0.20, 0.32, 0.50, 0.80, 1.25, 2.00, 3.15, 5.00, 5.00, 8.00\nQ2: 0.13, 0.20, 0.32, 0.50, 0.80, 1.28, 2.00, 3.20, 5.04, 8.00, 8.00, 12.80\nQ3, Q4: same\n\nRatio 160 block:\nQ1: 0.10, 0.16, 0.25, 0.39, 0.63, 1.00, 1.56, 2.50, 3.94, 6.25, 6.25, 10.00\nQ2: 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.63, 1.00, 1.60, 2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 16.00\nQ3, Q4: same\n\nRatio 100 block:\nQ1: 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.63, 1.00, 1.60, 2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 16.00\nQ2: 0.26, 0.40, 0.64, 1.01, 1.60, 2.56, 4.00, 6.40, 10.08, 16.00, 16.00, 25.60\nQ3, Q4: same\n\nRatio 50 block:\nQ1: 0.32, 0.50, 0.80, 1.26, 2.00, 3.20, 5.00, 8.00, 12.60, 20.00, 20.00, 32.00\nQ2: 0.51, 0.80, 1.28, 2.02, 3.20, 5.12, 8.00, 12.80, 20.16, 32.00, 32.00, 51.20\nQ3, Q4: same\n\nDN order: 25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300.\n\nNote there's a typo in the markdown: \"Model number: MAX2\" on page 5 — it's MAGX2. I'll keep MAGX2.\n\nAlso note first table has a broken cell: `<td>1.58</td><td>1.00...` wait: Q1 ratio 400: DN125 = 1.00, DN150 = 1.58? Wait: 630/400 = 1.575 ≈ 1.58. Yes DN150 Q1=1.58. And in the markdown: `<td>1.00</td><td>1.58</td>` — order: DN100=0.63, DN125=1.00, DN150=1.58. ✓.\n\nWait, check ratio 400 Q1 row: values are 0.04 (25), 0.06 (32), 0.10 (40), 0.16 (50), 0.25 (65), 0.40 (80), 0.63 (100), 1.00 (125), 1.58 (150), 2.50 (200), 2.50 (250), 4.00 (300). 1600/400=4.0 ✓. 1000/400=2.5 ✓. Good.\n\nRatio 250 Q1: 16/250=0.064≈0.06, 25/250=0.1, 40/250=0.16, 63/250=0.252≈0.25, 100/250=0.4, 160/250=0.64, 250/250=1.0, 400/250=1.6, 630/250=2.52, 1000/250=4.0, 4.0, 1600/250=6.4 ✓.\n\nRatio 200 Q1: 16/200=0.08, 25/200=0.125≈0.13, 40/200=0.2, 63/200=0.315≈0.32, 0.5, 0.8, 1.25, 2.0, 3.15, 5.0, 5.0, 8.0 ✓.\n\nRatio 160 Q1: 16/160=0.1, 25/160=0.156≈0.16, 40/160=0.25, 63/160=0.39375≈0.39, 100/160=0.625≈0.63, 1.0, 250/160=1.5625≈1.56, 2.5, 630/160=3.9375≈3.94, 6.25, 6.25, 10.0 ✓.\n\nRatio 100 Q1: 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.63, 1.00, 1.60, 2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 16.00 ✓.\n\nRatio 50 Q1: 16/50=0.32, 25/50=0.5, 40/50=0.8, 63/50=1.26, 2.0, 3.2, 5.0, 8.0, 12.6, 20.0, 20.0, 32.0 ✓.\n\nNow lengths: DN25–80: 200 mm; DN100, DN125: 250 mm; DN150: 300 mm. Wait let me re-check colspans: `<td>Length of horizontal water meter L [mm]</td><td colspan=\"6\">200</td><td colspan=\"2\">250</td><td>300</td>`. The header row has 9 DN columns. 6 → DN25,32,40,50,65,80 = 200. 2 → DN100,125 = 250. 1 → DN150 = 300. Hmm, but typically DN150 = 300? ISO 4064 length for DN150 is 300? Common flange lengths: DN100=250, DN150=300? Hmm, ISO lengths: DN50=200, DN80=200 or 250, DN100=250, DN150=300? Actually per ISO 4064 Table: DN100 → 250, DN150 → 300? There are series. Fine, take it as parsed.\n\nSecond table: DN200: 350, DN250: 400, DN300: 500.\n\nIndicating range: DN25,32,40,50 (colspan 4): 99999 m³; DN65–150 (colspan 5): 999999 m³; DN200–300: 9999999 m³.\n\nNow type-level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: 2 (this also goes in recommendation.accuracy_classes)\n- mpe_lower_zone: ±5% → value... MPE as ±5% — I can represent value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\". But ±5% is a limit; value could be {min: -5, max: 5}? Simpler: value \"±5\" hmm. Rules say numbers not strings. MPE ±5% → I'd write value: 5, unit: \"%\", attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone\". Or use string \"±5\". I'll use value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\" and attribute name conveys max permissible error. Hmm, ±... maybe {min:-5, max:5}. I'll go with 5 since MPE is a magnitude limit. Actually, to be safe and lossless, keep as string \"±5\"? Rule 2 says numbers not strings for numbers, but ± is a sign construct. I think value: 5, unit \"%\" is fine.\n\n- mpe_upper_zone: two temperature conditions — config_level with axis \"water_temperature\": ±2% for ≤30°C, ±3% for >30°C. I'll use config_level: attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_zone\", axis \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"water temperature ≤ 30°C\", value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"water temperature > 30°C\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}].\n\n- temperature_class: T50 (value: \"T50\")\n- pressure_loss_class: ΔP 10 → value \"ΔP10\"? The marking says \"Δp 10\". I'll record pressure_loss_class: \"ΔP10\". Keep as string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_classes: \"U5 D3\"\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- connection_type: \"flange\"\n- climatic_environment_class: \"B\"\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E2\"\n- software_version: \"21.39\"\n- checksum: \"09585\"\n- power_supply: split into:\n  - power_supply_voltage_ac: {min: 90, max: 250}, unit \"V\" (VAC) — attribute \"power_supply_voltage_ac\", unit_symbol \"V\". Also power_supply_type AC.\n  - power_supply_frequency: 50 Hz\n  - power_supply_voltage_dc: {min: 12, max: 36}, unit \"V\"\n  Or a single structured: power_supply_voltage with two configs. I'll do:\n  - \"power_supply_voltage_ac\": {value: {min:90, max:250}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - \"power_supply_frequency\": {value: 50, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n  - \"power_supply_voltage_dc\": {value: {min:12, max:36}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- low_flow_cutoff: 1% of nominal flowrate → value 1, unit \"%\". Attribute \"low_flow_cutoff\", maybe add reference. I'll keep value 1, unit \"%\" and attribute name low_flow_cutoff_of_nominal_flowrate. Hmm, or characteristic low_flow_cutoff: {value: 1, unit_symbol: \"%\"} with description. I'll name it \"low_flow_cutoff\" and value 1 \"%\". Maybe note reference \"nominal flowrate\" as separate? Rule 3's example: separate characteristic safe_overload_reference. I could omit; or include \"low_flow_cutoff_reference\": \"nominal flowrate\". I'll skip the reference; name attribute low_flow_cutoff_percent_of_nominal_flowrate? Cleaner: attribute \"low_flow_cutoff\", value 1, unit \"%\". Hmm the rule example suggests adding a reference characteristic \"(Or omit if too noisy.)\" I'll omit.\n\n- resolution of indicating device: 0.001 m³ normal mode; 0.000001 m³ calibration mode. config_level with axis \"mode\": [{condition \"normal mode\", value 0.001, unit \"m³\"}, {condition \"calibration mode\", value 0.000001, unit \"m³\"}]. \n\n- ratios: q2_q1_ratio: 1.6 (type-level, applies to all). q4_q3_ratio: 1.25. q3_q1_ratio: options 400/250/200/160/100/50 → this is a configuration axis. config_level: attribute \"q3_q1_ratio\", axis \"meter_configuration\", values for each of 6 ratios? Or just a type-level list? Rules say lists of values (like Emax list) are model matrix. Here ratio is a config option — config_level with axis \"q3_q1_ratio\" whose values enumerate the options: condition \" selectable configuration\", hmm. Actually simpler: characteristics type_level \"q3_q1_ratio_options\": {value: \"400, 250, 200, 160, 100, 50\"}? No — rule says lists become model matrices... but this is genuinely a set of allowed configurations, not per-model variation. I'll use config_level: attribute \"q3_q1_ratio\", axis \"nominal ratio ( selectable)\", values [{condition: \"R400\", value: 400}, {condition: \"R250\", value: 250}, ...]. That captures the 6 options.\n\nHmm wait — but then Q1/Q2 config_level values are keyed by DN AND ratio. Let me reconsider: maybe simpler to encode condition as \"DN25, R400\" etc. in config_level for q1 and q2. 144 entries total. That's big but complete. Combined with matrix_tables duplication... it's redundant but the schema encourages both (R137 example has matrix_tables AND model_level). I'll include both.\n\nActually, let me reconsider size. 72 rows in matrix table + 144 config values = manageable JSON, maybe ~15-20KB. Fine.\n\nHmm, alternatively I could make q1/q2 model_level with 72 values each keyed by condition... no, config_level is designed for this.\n\nLet me decide the model_level entries:\n- nominal_diameter (mm): 25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300\n- permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h): 16.0, 25.0, 40.0, 63.0, 100.0, 160.0, 250.0, 400.0, 630.0, 1000.0, 1000.0, 1600.0\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h): 20.0, 31.3, 50.0, 78.8, 125.0, 200.0, 312.5, 500.0, 787.5, 1250.0, 1250.0, 2000.0\n- length (mm): 200×6, 250 (DN100, DN125), 300 (DN150), 350, 400, 500\n- indicating_range (m³): 99999 (DN25-50), 999999 (DN65-150), 9999999 (DN200-300)\n\nModel IDs: \"MAGX2 DN25\", \"MAGX2 DN32\", ..., \"MAGX2 DN300\". Model family name: \"MAGX2\".\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"MAGX2\"]\n- module_designation: null? The certified item is a complete water meter (measuring system incl. calculating/indicating device). I'd say null or \"Not applicable\". The certificate describes the whole meter. I'll put null... Actually rule: module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. For a complete instrument, \"Not applicable\" seems right. I'll use null to avoid fabricating. Hmm. The cert covers a complete water meter — no module split. I'll put \"Not applicable\"? Safer: omit/null. I'll set null.\n\nActually let me include description text summarizing.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-CZ-16.04\"\n- scheme: hmm, scheme A|B. This is a \"BASIC CERTIFICATE OF CONFORMITY\" — OIML Basic Certificate corresponds to scheme... In OIML-CS, Basic Certificates are issued under Scheme B? Actually: OIML-CS has two schemes: Scheme A (Certificate of Conformity issued by Issuing Authorities of Member States) and Scheme B (Basic Certificate... ). Wait — actually it's reversed: Scheme A is \"OIML Certificate of Conformity\" issued by Issuing Authority using OIML type evaluation results; Scheme B is \"OIML Basic Certificate of Conformity\" based on... Hmm.\n\nLet me recall: OIML-CS introduced 2017 with Scheme A (certificates issued by Issuing Authorities, formerly the OIML Certificate System) and Scheme B (Basic Certificates issued by Issuing Authorities... usingBasic Evaluation Reports?).\n\nFrom OIML B 15 / OIML-CS docs: \"Scheme A: OIML Certificate of Conformity ... issued by an OIML Issuing Authority\". \"Scheme B: OIML Basic Certificate of Conformity ... issued by an OIML Issuing Authority ... based on a Basic Type Evaluation Report\". Hmm, I believe Scheme A = national certificates under MAA? No...\n\nActually correct: OIML-CS (since 2017):\n- Scheme A: Issuing Authorities issue OIML Certificates of Conformity (like the traditional system).\n- Scheme B: Issuing Authorities issue OIML Basic Certificates of Conformity, which can lead to national certifications by Member States using the associated Basic Type Evaluation Report.\n\nThis document is titled \"OIML BASIC CERTIFICATE OF CONFORMITY\" → scheme B.\n\nThe schema says scheme (A|B). So \"B\".\n\n- project_number: not present. Omit.\n- page_total: 8\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"2017-10-12\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not stated. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Czech Metrology Institute\"\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"CZ\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\"\n- person_title: not given. Omit.\n- The signature: \"The OIML Issuing Authority: Pavel Klenovský\". That's the signer. person_responsible is Jan Kalandra per the label. I could note signer... schema only has person_responsible. Keep Jan Kalandra. Maybe add Pavel Klenovský? The schema field person_responsible — the label \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\" is explicit. I'll keep that.\n- oiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\napplicants: [{name: \"Arkon Flow Systems, s.r.o.\", address_lines: [\"Berkova 534/92\", \"612 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same.\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null/omit\n- scheme: \"B\"? The recommendation scheme... schema has scheme in recommendation too. The certificate is scheme B. I'll include scheme: \"B\" at certificate level and maybe omit in recommendation or also \"B\". I'll include in recommendation too as \"B\"? Hmm, ambiguous — recommendation scheme typically refers to which scheme the certificate is under. I'll include scheme: \"B\" in both.\n- accuracy_classes: [\"2\"] or [2]? Accuracy class 2. I'll use [2] as number? Schema example not given; strings safer: [\"2\"]. Hmm. I'll use [\"2\"].\n\ntest_reports: 7 reports, role \"type_evaluation_report\"? They're called \"Test report\" — role options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. They're associated test reports for the basic type evaluation. The cert says \"the associated OIML Basic Type Evaluation Report(s)\". So role: \"type_evaluation_report\" for all? They're labeled \"Test report No. ...\" and represent the type evaluation. I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\n1. id: \"6015-PT-P3021-16\", date: \"2016-08-17\", pages: 240\n2. id: \"8551-PT-E0122-16\", date: \"2016-05-30\", pages: 42\n3. id: \"8551-PT-E0142-16\", date: \"2016-07-10\", pages: 48\n4. id: \"6015-PT-P3039-17\", date: \"2017-09-12\", pages: 23\n5. id: \"8553-PT-P1005-17\", date: \"2017-02-07\", pages: 6\n6. id: \"8551-PT-E0152-17\", date: \"2017-07-12\", pages: 44\n7. id: \"8551-PT-E0153-17\", date: \"2017-08-27\", pages: 44\n\nrevision_history: [{revision: \"1\", date: null, changes: \"Revision 1\"}]. Date not given for revision. The certificate date issued 12 Oct 2017. Revision 1 is printed on every page. I'll include revision \"1\" with changes null or \"Revision 1 of certificate\". Hmm — don't fabricate. revision_history entry: {revision: \"1\", date: null, changes: null}. Maybe better to just note revision 1. I'll include {revision: \"1\", date: null, changes: \"Certificate revision 1\"}. Eh, changes should describe changes — unknown. I'll put changes: null.\n\nfootnotes: none present. Omit.\n\nComponents: The water meter consists of flow sensor and calculating/indicating device (compact or remote). The cert mentions \"connecting flow sensor and indicating device in case of compact version\". Could record components: flow sensor (part of MAGX2) and indicating device. But no separate type designations given (label shows \"MAGX2 IP68 - T230CMN - D25PT40SS\" for sensor?). That's from a photo. I think it's safer to omit components or include compact/remote versions as config axis. I'll add config_level axis \"version\": compact vs remote with characteristic like \"max_cable_length\" 6 m for remote version. Description mentions: \"There are two modifications: compact and remote version.\" and \"The maximum cable length for remote version is 6 meters.\"\n\nconfig_level entry: attribute \"maximum_cable_length\", axis \"version\", values: [{condition: \"remote\", value: 6, unit_symbol: \"m\"}]. Compact: not applicable — omit.\n\nMatrix tables:\n1. name: \"Basic technical data by nominal diameter (DN25–DN150)\" — columns: nominal_diameter_mm, indicating_range_m3, length_mm. Rows for DN25..DN150.\n\nActually I can merge both tables into one: \"Basic technical data by nominal diameter\" with rows DN25..DN300, columns: nominal_diameter_mm, indicating_range_m3, length_mm. \n\n2. name: \"Flowrates by nominal diameter and Q3/Q1 ratio\" — flat 72 rows: columns: nominal_diameter_mm, q3_q1_ratio, q1_m3_h, q2_m3_h, q3_m3_h, q4_m3_h.\n\nLet me now build all the data.\n\nDN list: [25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300]\nQ3: [16.0, 25.0, 40.0, 63.0, 100.0, 160.0, 250.0, 400.0, 630.0, 1000.0, 1000.0, 1600.0]\nQ4: [20.0, 31.3, 50.0, 78.8, 125.0, 200.0, 312.5, 500.0, 787.5, 1250.0, 1250.0, 2000.0]\nLength: {25:200, 32:200, 40:200, 50:200, 65:200, 80:200, 100:250, 125:250, 150:300, 200:350, 250:400, 300:500}\nIndicating range: {25:99999, 32:99999, 40:99999, 50:99999, 65:999999, 80:999999, 100:999999, 125:999999, 150:999999, 200:9999999, 250:9999999, 300:9999999}\n\nQ1 by ratio:\nR400: [0.04, 0.06, 0.10, 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.63, 1.00, 1.58, 2.50, 2.50, 4.00]\nR250: [0.06, 0.10, 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.64, 1.00, 1.60, 2.52, 4.00, 4.00, 6.40]\nR200: [0.08, 0.13, 0.20, 0.32, 0.50, 0.80, 1.25, 2.00, 3.15, 5.00, 5.00, 8.00]\nR160: [0.10, 0.16, 0.25, 0.39, 0.63, 1.00, 1.56, 2.50, 3.94, 6.25, 6.25, 10.00]\nR100: [0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.63, 1.00, 1.60, 2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 16.00]\nR50:  [0.32, 0.50, 0.80, 1.26, 2.00, 3.20, 5.00, 8.00, 12.60, 20.00, 20.00, 32.00]\n\nQ2 by ratio:\nR400: [0.06, 0.10, 0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.64, 1.00, 1.60, 2.52, 4.00, 4.00, 6.40]\nR250: [0.10, 0.16, 0.26, 0.40, 0.64, 1.02, 1.60, 2.56, 4.03, 6.40, 6.40, 10.24]\nR200: [0.13, 0.20, 0.32, 0.50, 0.80, 1.28, 2.00, 3.20, 5.04, 8.00, 8.00, 12.80]\nR160: [0.16, 0.25, 0.40, 0.63, 1.00, 1.60, 2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 16.00]\nR100: [0.26, 0.40, 0.64, 1.01, 1.60, 2.56, 4.00, 6.40, 10.08, 16.00, 16.00, 25.60]\nR50:  [0.51, 0.80, 1.28, 2.02, 3.20, 5.12, 8.00, 12.80, 20.16, 32.00, 32.00, 51.20]\n\nModel IDs: I'll use \"DN25\", \"DN32\", ... or \"MAGX2 DN25\"? Use \"MAGX2-DN25\"? The schema example uses \"Emax=25t\", \"G1.6\". I'll use \"DN25\" style with model_id like \"MAGX2 DN25\". Let me use \"MAGX2 DN25\" etc. to be explicit.\n\nNow config_level for q1/q2: conditions \"MAGX2 DN25, R400\"? The value entries: {\"condition\": \"MAGX2 DN25, ratio Q3/Q1=400\", \"value\": 0.04, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"}. Hmm the structured value form for config_level per rule 5: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`. OK.\n\n144 entries... let me just write them systematically. Actually, wait — do I want to duplicate everything in both matrix_tables AND config_level? The R137 example shows duplication between matrix_tables and model_level is expected. OK, I'll do it.\n\nLet me also double check Q2 for R400 DN150: 2.52 (1.58×1.6=2.528). ✓ given as 2.52.\n\nNow the characteristics.type_level:\n- accuracy_class: {value: 2, unit_symbol: null}\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrate_zone: {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — hmm ±5%. I'll keep value 5. Or maybe represent as string \"±5\"? Rule: numbers not strings. ±5 as {min:-5,max:5} is overthinking. I'll use 5.\n- temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- pressure_loss_class: {value: \"ΔP10\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_classes: {value: \"U5 D3\"}\n- orientation_limitation: {value: \"any\"}\n- connection_type: {value: \"flange\"}\n- climatic_environment_class: {value: \"B\"}\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: {value: \"E2\"}\n- software_version: {value: \"21.39\"}\n- checksum: {value: \"