ls: [{\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN25\", \"label\": \"DN25\", ...}, ...] Maybe include attributes like nominal diameter in attributes? Not needed since model_level covers. Keep models simple with model_id and label.\n\ncertified_type.description: Summarize: electromagnetic water meter for cold and hot potable water, compact and remote versions, inductive principle, PTFE and hard rubber lining, straight inlet 5×DN and outlet 3×DN, without flow conditioner, electronic calculating/indicating device, measures volume (positive, negative, total, auxiliary) in m³ and flow rate in m³/h, not designed for reverse flow, optional frequency output or RS 485 communication, DN25–DN300, flanged.\n\nAlso software version 21.39 checksum 09585 — captured in type_level.\n\nMarking info — could include as part of description; skip detail.\n\nNow certificate.page_total: 8.\n\ndate_issued: \"2017-10-12\" (date on signature page 2: 12 October 2017).\n\nLet me also consider \"For further characteristics see page 2 to 7\" — ignore.\n\nPower supply: \"(90 – 250) VAC / 50 Hz, (12 – 36) VDC\". I'll add power_supply_type entries? I have voltage_ac (V AC), voltage_dc (V DC), frequency 50 Hz. Good.\n\nLow flow cut off: 1% from nominal flowrate: {value: 1, unit_symbol: \"%\"} attribute \"low_flow_cutoff\". Maybe note reference in attribute name: \"low_flow_cutoff_percent_of_nominal_flowrate\"? Hmm, canonical labels should be simple. I'll use \"low_flow_cutoff\" and add separate \"low_flow_cutoff_reference\": {\"value\": \"nominal flowrate\"}? The rule example did exactly this pattern for safe_overload. I'll add it — small cost. Actually \"1 % from nominal flowrate\" — the reference is nominal flowrate. I'll include low_flow_cutoff: 1% and omit reference (allowed \"or omit\"). I'll omit to keep clean. Hmm... Actually including is more faithful. I'll include it. Eh — I'll skip; attribute name \"low_flow_cutoff\" with unit % is clear enough.\n\nAlso \"Indicating range\" is per model (varies) → model_level. ✓\n\nTemperature class T50: means 0.1–50°C for hot water meters per R49? T50 covers cold 0.1–30 and hot up to 50. Don't fabricate — just T50.\n\nNow matrix_tables:\n\nTable 1: name \"Basic technical data by nominal diameter\"\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"indicating_range\", \"length\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³\", \"mm\"]\nrows: 12 rows:\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 25, \"indicating_range\": 99999, \"length\": 200}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 32, \"indicating_range\": 99999, \"length\": 200}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 40, \"indicating_range\": 99999, \"length\": 200}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"indicating_range\": 99999, \"length\": 200}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 65, \"indicating_range\": 999999, \"length\": 200}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 80, \"indicating_range\": 999999, \"length\": 200}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 100, \"indicating_range\": 999999, \"length\": 250}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 125, \"indicating_range\": 999999, \"length\": 250}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 150, \"indicating_range\": 999999, \"length\": 300}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 200, \"indicating_range\": 9999999, \"length\": 350}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 250, \"indicating_range\": 9999999, \"length\": 400}\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 300, \"indicating_range\": 9999999, \"length\": 500}\n\nTable 2: name \"Flowrates by nominal diameter and Q3/Q1 ratio\"\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\"]\nrows: 72 rows. Let me enumerate.\n\nFor each ratio in [400, 250, 200, 160, 100, 50], for each DN index:\n\nR400:\n25: q1 0.04, q2 0.06, q3 16.0, q4 20.0\n32: 0.06, 0.10, 25.0, 31.3\n40: 0.10, 0.16, 40.0, 50.0\n50: 0.16, 0.25, 63.0, 78.8\n65: 0.25, 0.40, 100.0, 125.0\n80: 0.40, 0.64, 160.0, 200.0\n100: 0.63, 1.00, 250.0, 312.5\n125: 1.00, 1.60, 400.0, 500.0\n150: 1.58, 2.52, 630.0, 787.5\n200: 2.50, 4.00, 1000.0, 1250.0\n250: 2.50, 4.00, 1000.0, 1250.0\n300: 4.00, 6.40, 1600.0, 2000.0\n\nR250:\n25: 0.06, 0.10\n32: 0.10, 0.16\n40: 0.16, 0.26\n50: 0.25, 0.40\n65: 0.40, 0.64\n80: 0.64, 1.02\n100: 1.00, 1.60\n125: 1.60, 2.56\n150: 2.52, 4.03\n200: 4.00, 6.40\n250: 4.00, 6.40\n300: 6.40, 10.24\n\nR200:\n25: 0.08, 0.13\n32: 0.13, 0.20\n40: 0.20, 0.32\n50: 0.32, 0.50\n65: 0.50, 0.80\n80: 0.80, 1.28\n100: 1.25, 2.00\n125: 2.00, 3.20\n150: 3.15, 5.04\n200: 5.00, 8.00\n250: 5.00, 8.00\n300: 8.00, 12.80\n\nR160:\n25: 0.10, 0.16\n32: 0.16, 0.25\n40: 0.25, 0.40\n50: 0.39, 0.63\n65: 0.63, 1.00\n80: 1.00, 1.60\n100: 1.56, 2.50\n125: 2.50, 4.00\n150: 3.94, 6.30\n200: 6.25, 10.00\n250: 6.25, 10.00\n300: 10.00, 16.00\n\nR100:\n25: 0.16, 0.26\n32: 0.25, 0.40\n40: 0.40, 0.64\n50: 0.63, 1.01\n65: 1.00, 1.60\n80: 1.60, 2.56\n100: 2.50, 4.00\n125: 4.00, 6.40\n150: 6.30, 10.08\n200: 10.00, 16.00\n250: 10.00, 16.00\n300: 16.00, 25.60\n\nR50:\n25: 0.32, 0.51\n32: 0.50, 0.80\n40: 0.80, 1.28\n50: 1.26, 2.02\n65: 2.00, 3.20\n80: 3.20, 5.12\n100: 5.00, 8.00\n125: 8.00, 12.80\n150: 12.60, 20.16\n200: 20.00, 32.00\n250: 20.00, 32.00\n300: 32.00, 51.20\n\nq3/q4 same across ratios.\n\nNow write config_level q1 entries with condition \"DN25, R400\" etc. — 72 per attribute.\n\nHmm, this is getting really long. Should I include both matrix table AND config entries? The instructions emphasize structured extraction. The R137 example did both matrix_tables and model_level. I'll do both for completeness. It's long but correct.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe trim: config_level for q1/q2 with 72 entries each. Alternatively, since the axis is q3_q1_ratio and the conditions encode both DN and ratio, this is somewhat abusing config_level. But it's the best fit. OK, include.\n\nAlternatively — could I treat the ratio as part of the model? i.e., models = 72: \"MAGX2 DN25 R400\" etc. Then q1, q2, q3, q4, DN all become model_level attributes with 72 values each. That's also 72×5 entries... similar size but cleaner semantics? The certificate treats ratio as an option (\"400 or 250 or 200 or 160 or 100 or 50\"), not distinct type variants. I'll stick with DN models + ratio config axis.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. nominal_diameter, unit mm, 12 values\n2. permanent_flowrate_q3, m³/h, 12\n3. overload_flowrate_q4, m³/h, 12\n4. length, mm, 12 — attribute \"length_of_horizontal_water_meter\" → canonical \"length\"? I'll use \"length\".\n5. indicating_range, m³, 12\n\nLet me also double-check the \"Indicating range\" interpretation: \"Indicating range[m³]: 99 999\" for DN25-50 and \"999 999\" for DN65-150. Values with spaces — 99999 and 999999. Yes.\n\nWait, actually re-check colspans on page 3: `<td>Indicating range[m³]</td><td colspan=\"4\">99 999</td><td colspan=\"5\">999 999</td>`. 4+5=9 columns ✓. DN25,32,40,50 → 99 999; DN65,80,100,125,150 → 999 999.\n\nHmm interesting, but DN50 Q3=63, so max volume indication 99999 m³ vs annual volume... fine.\n\nNow check Length colspans again: colspan 6 → DN25-80 = 200; colspan 2 → DN100,125 = 250; colspan 1 → DN150 = 300. Hmm wait, DN150 length 300, DN100/DN125 length 250? Standard ISO 4064-1 Annex normative lengths: DN100 = 250, DN125 = 250, DN150 = 300? Actually standard lengths: N100=250, N125=250, N150=300? ISO 4064: DN100→250, DN150→300, DN125→250. Yes that's right (series T? whatever). OK.\n\nSecond table DN200: 350, DN250: 400, DN300: 500. ISO: DN200→350, DN250→400, DN300→500 ✓.\n\nNow build JSON.\n\ncertificate:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-CZ-16.04\",\n  \"scheme\": \"B\",\n  \"page_total\": 8,\n  \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2017-10-12\"\n}\n\nissuing_authority:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Czech Metrology Institute\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"CZ\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"Jan Kalandra\"\n}\n\nMaybe add \"Pavel Klenovský\" as signer — schema doesn't have signer field; person_title... skip.\n\napplicants: one entry.\nmanufacturers: one entry.\n\ncertified_type:\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"MAGX2\"],\n  \"module_designation\": null,\n  \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meter for metering cold and hot potable water, based on an inductive principle, with PTFE and hard rubber lining, straight inlet (5 times the diameter) and outlet (3 times the diameter) length, without flow conditioner, equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device. Two modifications: compact and remote version (maximum cable length 6 m for remote). Display shows volume (positive, negative, total and auxiliary) in m³ and flow rate in m³/h; not designed to measure reverse flow. Optional frequency output or RS 485 communication. DN25 to DN300, flanged connections.\"\n}\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"MAGX2\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN25\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN32\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN40\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN50\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN65\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN80\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN100\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN125\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN150\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN200\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN250\"}, {\"model_id\": \"MAGX2 DN300\"}\n  ]\n}\n\ncharacteristics:\ntype_level: as above.\n\nLet me write:\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n  ...\n}\n\nShould I include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] everywhere? The schema example shows footnote_markers in the structured value. I'll include unit_symbol and omit unit_id/footnote_markers when empty to keep it cleaner? Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with that shape including unit_id and footnote_markers fields? The shape shows those keys. \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\". I'll include unit_id: null always, and footnote_markers only when non-empty? The shape includes footnote_markers: [\"1)\"] — I'll include footnote_markers: [] when empty? Hmm, that bloats. The shape mandates it (\"MUST be an object with this shape\"). I'll include all three keys: value, unit_symbol, unit_